---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeCycleCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/UnitsLifeCycleCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="period-calculator">
  <UnitsLifeCycleCalculator client:load mode="period" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Six cycles ahead, on one printable page</h2>
    <p>
      The arithmetic is simple: add your cycle length to the first day of the last period, and
      repeat. But six times in your head, across month boundaries and a leap year, it is not simple.
      This period calculator projects six cycles, marks them on a calendar and
      prints cleanly, which is what you actually want when you are booking a holiday, a wedding, a
      race or a medical procedure.
    </p>

    <h2>How cycle length is measured</h2>
    <p>
      From the <strong>first day of bleeding</strong> in one period to the day before bleeding
      starts in the next. Not from the day bleeding stops. Getting this wrong shortens every
      prediction by the length of your period, usually four to six days. That makes the whole
      projection useless.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your cycles vary, use the average of the last three to six, and treat the prediction as a
      centre point with a few days either side rather than a date.
    </p>

    <h2>What counts as normal</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Typical range</th><th scope="col">Worth mentioning to a clinician</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cycle length, adults</td><td>21&ndash;35 days</td><td>Consistently under 21 or over 35</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cycle length, first years after menarche</td><td>21&ndash;45 days</td><td>Still irregular after about three years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Period length</td><td>2&ndash;7 days</td><td>Over 7 days, or a sudden change</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Variation between cycles</td><td>Up to 7&ndash;9 days</td><td>Sudden new irregularity</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bleeding</td><td>Manageable with normal products</td><td>Soaking through hourly, or clots larger than a 10p coin</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Bleeding between periods, after sex, or after menopause is always worth getting checked,
      regardless of what any calendar says.
    </p>

    <h2>The premenstrual week</h2>
    <p>
      The amber band on the calendar is the seven days before the next period is due: the late
      luteal phase, when progesterone falls and premenstrual symptoms are most common. About 3 in 4 people
      who menstruate report some premenstrual symptoms; a much smaller group meet the criteria for
      premenstrual dysphoric disorder, which is a recognised diagnosis with specific treatments and
      is worth raising with a clinician rather than enduring.
    </p>
    <p>
      Symptoms that begin at ovulation rather than a week before, or that do not stop when bleeding
      starts, are usually something other than PMS.
    </p>

    <h2>Things that shift a cycle</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Illness, particularly with fever, in the first half of the cycle</li>
      <li>Significant travel across time zones, and sustained sleep disruption</li>
      <li>Sharp changes in training load or body weight</li>
      <li>Acute stress, which usually delays ovulation, not the period itself</li>
      <li>Starting, stopping or missing hormonal contraception</li>
      <li>Thyroid conditions and PCOS</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Almost all of these act by delaying ovulation. Because the luteal phase is fixed, a delayed
      ovulation delays the period by exactly the same number of days. So this period calculator also
      shows the fertile window, because it explains the timing.
    </p>

    <h2>A tracker that cannot leak</h2>
    <p>
      Period-tracking apps have had repeated data-sharing scandals and regulatory action, because
      they collect intimate data on a server and make money from it. Here the dates stay in the page
      while you use it and are discarded when you close the tab. If you switch on local saving, they
      go to your own browser only, with a delete button next to the switch.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
